NNAAMMEE
       ibv_fork_init - initialize libibverbs to support fork()

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ##iinncclluuddee <<iinnffiinniibbaanndd//vveerrbbss..hh>>

       iinntt iibbvv__ffoorrkk__iinniitt((vvooiidd));;

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       iibbvv__ffoorrkk__iinniitt(())  initializes  libibverbs's  data  structures  to handle
       ffoorrkk(()) function calls correctly  and  avoid  data  corruption,  whether
       ffoorrkk(()) is called explicitly or implicitly (such as in ssyysstteemm(())).

       It  is not necessary to use this function if all parent process threads
       are always blocked until all child processes end or change address spa‐
       ces via an eexxeecc(()) operation.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       iibbvv__ffoorrkk__iinniitt(())  returns 0 on success, or the value of errno on failure
       (which indicates the failure reason).

NNOOTTEESS
       iibbvv__ffoorrkk__iinniitt(()) works on Linux  kernels  supporting  the  MMAADDVV__DDOONNTTFFOORRKK
       flag for mmaaddvviissee(()) (2.6.17 and higher).

       Setting  the  environment variable RRDDMMAAVV__FFOORRKK__SSAAFFEE or IIBBVV__FFOORRKK__SSAAFFEE has
       the same effect as calling iibbvv__ffoorrkk__iinniitt(()).

       Setting the environment variable RRDDMMAAVV__HHUUGGEEPPAAGGEESS__SSAAFFEE tells the library
       to  check  the  underlying  page size used by the kernel for memory re‐
       gions.  This is required if an application uses huge pages  either  di‐
       rectly or indirectly via a library such as libhugetlbfs.

       Calling  iibbvv__ffoorrkk__iinniitt(()) will reduce performance due to an extra system
       call for every memory registration, and the additional memory allocated
       to track memory regions.  The precise performance impact depends on the
       workload and usually will not be significant.

       Setting RRDDMMAAVV__HHUUGGEEPPAAGGEESS__SSAAFFEE adds further overhead to all memory regis‐
       trations.

